[[Old Chinese]] had a simpler system with no palatal or retroflex consonants; the more complex system of EMC is thought to have arisen from a combination of Old Chinese obstruents with a following {{IPA|/r/}} and/or {{IPA|/j/}}.{{sfnp|Baxter|1992|pp=177–179}}

[[Bernhard Karlgren]] developed the [[Karlgren–Li reconstruction of Middle Chinese|first modern reconstruction of Middle Chinese]]. The main differences between Karlgren and newer reconstructions of the initials are:
* The reversal of {{IPA|/ʑ/}} and {{IPA|/dʑ/}}. Karlgren based his reconstruction on the [[Song dynasty]] [[rime table]]s. However, because of mergers between these two sounds between Early and Late Middle Chinese, the Chinese phonologists who created the rime tables could rely only on tradition to tell what the respective values of these two consonants were; evidently they were accidentally reversed at one stage.
* Karlgren also assumed that the EMC [[retroflex consonant|retroflex]] stops were actually [[palatal consonant|palatal]] stops based on their tendency to co-occur with front vowels and {{IPA|/j/}}, but this view is no longer held.
* Karlgren assumed that voiced consonants were actually [[breathy voiced]]. This is now assumed only for LMC, not EMC.

Other sources from around the same time as the ''Qieyun'' reveal a slightly different system, which is believed to reflect southern pronunciation. In this system, the voiced fricatives {{IPA|/z/}} and {{IPA|/ʐ/}} are not distinguished from the voiced affricates {{IPA|/dz/}} and {{IPA|/ɖʐ/}}, respectively, and the retroflex stops are not distinguished from the dental stops.{{sfnp|Pulleyblank|1984|p=144}}

Several changes occurred between the time of the ''Qieyun'' and the rime tables:
* Palatal sibilants merged with retroflex sibilants.{{sfnp|Baxter|1992|p=53}}
* {{IPA|/ʐ/}} merged with {{IPA|/ɖʐ/}} (hence reflecting four separate EMC phonemes).
* The palatal nasal {{IPA|/ɲ/}} also became retroflex, but turned into a new phoneme {{IPA|/r/}} rather than merging with any existing phoneme.
* The palatal allophone of {{IPA|/ɣ/}} ({{lang|zh-Hant|云}}) merged with {{IPA|/j/}} ({{lang|zh-Hant|以}}) as a single laryngeal initial {{IPA|/j/}} ({{lang|zh-Hant|喻}}).{{sfnp|Baxter|1992|pp=55–56, 59}}
* A new series of labiodentals emerged from labials in certain environments, typically where both fronting and rounding occurred (e.g. {{IPA|/j/}} plus a back vowel in William Baxter's reconstruction, or a [[front rounded vowel]] in Chan's reconstruction). However, modern [[Min Chinese|Min]] dialects retain bilabial initials in such words, while modern [[Hakka Chinese|Hakka]] dialects preserve them in some common words.{{sfnp|Baxter|1992|pp=46–48}}
* Voiced [[obstruent]]s gained phonetic [[breathy voice]] (still reflected in the [[Wu Chinese]] varieties).
